course: biology
lesson: 351
title: Teeth: baby teeth, grown-up teeth and wobbly teeth
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: You get two sets of teeth in your life: a small set of baby teeth for a small jaw, and a big set of grown-up teeth that has to last until you are old.
explain: Picture a dentist's X-ray of a seven-year-old: the baby teeth sit in the mouth, and underneath them, packed into the jaw like eggs in a box, the grown-up teeth are already waiting.
explain: When a grown-up tooth starts to push up, it dissolves the root of the baby tooth above it, so the baby tooth wobbles and falls out with hardly any root left, and that is why it barely hurts.
explain: Teeth look like bones, but they are not: the hard outside has no living cells, so a tooth cannot mend itself the way a broken arm does.
fact: A full set of baby teeth, also called milk teeth, has 20 teeth: ten on top and ten on the bottom.
fact: A full grown-up set has 32 teeth, including four wisdom teeth at the very back, but many people never get all four wisdom teeth and some have them taken out because there is no room.
fact: The first baby tooth usually comes through at about six months, often at the bottom front, and teething can make a baby grumpy and dribbly; about one baby in a few thousand is born with a tooth already showing.
fact: A wobbly baby tooth is loose because the grown-up tooth underneath has dissolved its root, and a new tooth usually comes through the gap within a few months.
fact: A child's jaw holds two sets at once: baby teeth in the mouth and grown-up teeth forming underneath, years before they come through.
fact: At about six, the first grown-up back teeth, the six-year molars, come in behind the baby teeth without any baby tooth falling out, so many people do not realise they are permanent.
fact: A grown-up set has 8 incisors for cutting, 4 canines for gripping, 8 premolars and 12 molars for grinding.
fact: Lions have huge canines for gripping prey, while cows have no top front teeth at all, only a hard pad to press grass against.
fact: In Britain and the USA the tooth fairy swaps a lost tooth for a coin; in Spain a mouse called Ratoncito Pérez does the job, and in some countries children throw the tooth onto the roof.
fact: People get only two sets of teeth, while sharks and crocodiles keep growing new ones all their lives, and elephants get six sets of big molars one after another.
fact: Teeth are not bones: they have no marrow, the enamel has no living cells, and a hole in a tooth cannot heal by itself.
exercise: How many teeth are in a full set of baby teeth? | 20 | 32 | 10 | 28
exercise: How many teeth are in a full grown-up set, wisdom teeth included? | 32 | 20 | 28 | 36
exercise: Where are the wisdom teeth? | At the back of the mouth | At the very front of the mouth | Under the tongue, out of sight | Hidden inside the cheeks, near the ears
exercise: At about what age does a baby's first tooth usually come through? | About six months | Only about two weeks | About three years | About one month before birth
exercise: Why does a baby tooth become wobbly? | The grown-up tooth underneath dissolves its root | The baby tooth slowly rots away from eating too many sweets | The gum shrinks when you grow tall | The tooth dries out and goes light
exercise: Why does a lost baby tooth look so short? | Its root has been dissolved almost away | Baby teeth never have any roots at all, not even short ones | The tooth fairy trims it | It broke off and the root stayed in
exercise: What does a dentist's X-ray of a child's jaw show? | Grown-up teeth waiting underneath the baby teeth | Only the baby teeth and empty bone | Just one big tooth in each jaw | The tongue muscles and nothing else
exercise: What is special about the six-year molars? | They are permanent teeth that come in with no baby tooth falling out | They are baby teeth that fall out again at seven | They are the wisdom teeth, arriving years and years early | They grow at the front instead of the back
exercise: What job do incisors do? | Cut and bite food | Grind food flat and smooth | Grip and tear meat | Hold the tongue still
exercise: Which teeth are best for grinding food? | Molars | Incisors | Canines | Milk teeth
exercise: In Spain, who swaps lost teeth for a gift? | A mouse called Ratoncito Pérez | A fairy called Pérez | A rabbit called the Tooth Bunny who hops in at night | A bird called the Tooth Stork
exercise: Which animal keeps growing new teeth all its life? | A shark | A human being | A dairy cow | A farm horse
exercise: How many sets of big molars does an elephant get in its life? | Six | Only two | Just one | Twenty
exercise: Are teeth bones? | No, they have no marrow and cannot mend themselves | Yes, they are the very hardest bones in the whole body | Yes, they are tiny finger bones | No, they are made of hardened skin
exercise: Why does a teething baby chew everything and dribble? | A tooth is pushing through the gum and it feels sore | The baby is very hungry all day long | The baby is copying grown-ups chewing gum | Its tongue is growing too big for its mouth
exercise: Why are the back teeth of a grown-up called wisdom teeth? | They come late, when a person is older and wiser | They help a person think and make them a lot cleverer | They grow from the brain downwards | Only wise people get them
test: «my sister's got all her baby teeth, how many's that» | 20 | 32 | 24 | 10
test: Count them: a boy had a full set of 20 baby teeth, lost four and no new ones have come yet. How many teeth are in his mouth? | 16 | 24 | 20 | 12
test: Count them: Dad has a full grown-up set with all his wisdom teeth. How many more teeth has he than a child with a full baby set? | 12 | 2 | 20 | 32
test: «mate says when a baby tooth falls out there's nothing under it, you just get a hole forever» What is right? | No, a grown-up tooth is already waiting underneath and comes through in a few months | Yes, the gap stays empty for life | Yes, unless you put the tooth back in | No, the same baby tooth grows back bigger
test: In a Tom and Jerry cartoon Tom gets hit, all his teeth fall out in a heap, and he pushes them back in and smiles. What would really happen to a grown-up cat or person? | Knocked-out adult teeth need a dentist fast, and lost ones do not grow back | The teeth click straight back in and work fine | New teeth grow back by the next morning | Teeth only fall out in cartoons, never in real life
test: «shark gets new teeth all the time, so do we innit, just keep losing em» What is right? | No, people get only two sets of teeth in a whole life | Yes, people grow a whole new set of teeth every ten years or so | Yes, but only if they drink milk | No, people get only one set
test: A girl of six finds a big new tooth at the back of her mouth and no gap where it came. What is the best advice? | It is a permanent molar, so brush it well because it must last for life | It is a baby tooth, so it does not matter much | It is a mistake and the dentist will pull it out | It is a wisdom tooth and will fall out soon
test: What would happen to an old elephant whose sixth and last set of molars wore out? | It could no longer chew its food properly | It would grow a seventh set straight away, then an eighth | It would start eating with its tusks | It would switch to drinking only water
test: «my tooth's wobbly and I'm scared, it'll hurt loads when it comes out yeah» What is the truth? | It barely hurts, because its root has already dissolved away | It hurts as much as a broken arm | It hurts unless you leave it in for years | It hurts badly, because the whole nerve gets pulled out along with it
test: «cow's got big fangs like a lion, it's a big animal innit» What is right? | No, a cow has no top front teeth, only a hard pad for pressing grass | Yes, a cow's fangs are even bigger than a lion's, because a cow is much heavier | Yes, all big animals have big fangs | No, cows have no teeth at all
test: «chipped my front tooth, it'll heal like a scraped knee right» What is right? | No, the enamel has no living cells, so a dentist must fix it | Yes, teeth heal in about a week | Yes, it grows back if you drink lots and lots of milk every day | No, the whole tooth will fall out now
exam: State the number of teeth comprised in a complete permanent dentition, the third molars included. | Thirty-two | Twenty | Twenty-eight | Thirty-six
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a deciduous tooth becomes loose prior to its loss. | The root thereof is dissolved by the permanent tooth developing beneath it | The tooth is weakened by excessive consumption of sugar | The gum recedes in consequence of growth in stature | The tooth desiccates and is rendered lighter
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between teeth and bones? | Teeth are not bones, possessing no marrow and being incapable of self-repair | Teeth are the hardest bones of the skeleton | Teeth are small bones which heal as the limbs do | Teeth are composed of hardened skin
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the number of sets of teeth acquired by a human being. | Two sets are acquired in the course of a lifetime | A new set is acquired at intervals of ten years | A single set is retained throughout life | Six successive sets of molars are acquired
